If one is interested in a command line version (non-excel, non-dbase;
no compiler required, just standard Unix awk, or GNU awk for windows)
here's a quick awk solution that prints both, the counts and the
histograms. The histogram is normalized depending on the actual data
to fit on 80 col screens. The program evaluates the dungeon level (if
the maximum dungeon level is preferred then change 4 to 5 in line 1).

    BEGIN { OFS="\t" ; lvl=4 }
    $lvl<=0 { $lvl=100-$lvl }
    { l[$lvl]++ ; if(l[$lvl]>max_cnt) max_cnt=l[$lvl] }
    $lvl>max_lvl { max_lvl=$lvl }
    END { print "Level","Deaths","Histo"
          for(i=1;i<=max_lvl;i++)
            if(i in l)
              print i,l[i],h(l[i]*60/max_cnt)
    }
    function h(n, s) { for(j=1;j<=n;j++) s=s"#"; return "|"s }

Put the program in a file called, say, dpl.awk and call it with the
Nethack logfile

  awk -f dpl.awk logfile

(As the C++ program the awk script does not distinguish deaths from
quits or escapes or ascensions.)

PS: I have no logfile at hand so it's not tested with real data.

